BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Last West: An evening exploring the making of Roadsongs for Doroth
 ea Lange
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T200000
UID:260904EIHpSDgdVEyRhD_eO0y_xQ
DESCRIPTION:As dusk falls over Western Hills Garden\, join poet and playwr
 ight Tess Taylor and director Ciera Eis for an intimate evening inside the
  making of *Last West: Roadsongs for Dorothea Lange.*\n\nHear readings fr
 om the play\, watch an unforgettable slideshow of Dorothea Lange's Califor
 nia photographs\, and go behind the scenes of an extraordinary production 
 in development.\n\nTess and Ciera will be in conversation about the images
 \, voices\, and stories at the heart of *Last West* — Lange's road jou
 rnals\, her Dust Bowl migrations\, her portraits of ordinary Californians 
 navigating water\, land\, work\, and belonging — and what her vision ask
 s of us now.\n\nThe evening will also open into a broader conversation abo
 ut the vital role spaces like Western Hills Garden play in sustaining arti
 sts and art-making in California today. Wine\, snacks\, and a light soup a
 nd salad supper included.\n\nJoin us for an evening of art\, conversation\
 , and California light bites including a simple soup\, salad\, and bread m
 eal. Space is limited.\n\nArtist Bios: **Tess Taylor** is a poet\, playwr
 ight\, and cultural critic\, and the author of five poetry collections inc
 luding *Last West: Roadsongs for Dorothea Lange*\, *Rift Zone*\, and *W
 ork & Days* (named one of the New York Times' 10 Best Poetry Books of 201
 6). Her criticism appears in Harper's Magazine\, The Atlantic\, and The Ne
 w York Times. She served as on-air poetry reviewer for NPR's All Things Co
 nsidered for over a decade and was awarded an Academy of American Poets La
 ureate Fellowship in 2024. Her sixth collection\, *Come Bite*\, is forthc
 oming from Milkweed Editions in April 2027.\n\n**Ciera Eis** is a bicoast
 al director who develops socio-political new work. Recent credits include
  *Last West: Roadsongs for Dorothea Lange* (Sonoma Valley Museum of Art\
 , Oakland Museum of California\, ColLABo at Carnegie Mellon University)\,
  *Hadestown* (American Conservatory Theatre YC)\, and *Love U: A New Mu
 sical* (Z Below). For five years she was a producer on Berkeley Repertory
 's New Works Summer Program\, The Ground Floor. She is a founding member o
 f the Bay Area Directors Collective and a 2024 Directors Lab West Recipien
 t.
LOCATION:Western Hills Garden
URL:https://www.westernhillsgarden.com/programs/last-west-2026
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
